<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piero di Lorenzo de' Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1472">1472</a>–<a href="/years/1503">1503</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Piero the Unfortunate · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piero di Lorenzo de' Medici (1472–1503), known as Piero the Unfortunate, was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a> and ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1492 to 1494. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-viii-of-france">Charles VIII of France</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1494, he surrendered key fortresses without the city's consent and was expelled with his family. He died in exile, drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano river</a> while serving with the French army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piero-di-lorenzo-de-medici.jpg" alt="Piero di Lorenzo de' Medici" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gherardo di Giovanni del Fora · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piero_di_Lorenzo_de_Medici_-_Domenico_Ghirlandaio.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Piero was born on 15 February 1472, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a> and Clarice Orsini. He was raised with his younger brother Giovanni, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>, and his cousin Giulio, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a>, and was educated to succeed his father by tutors such as Angelo Poliziano and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a>. His character, described as feeble, arrogant and undisciplined, proved unsuited to the role. Poliziano died of poisoning on 24 September 1494, very possibly at Piero's hand. In 1486 his uncle Bernardo Rucellai arranged his marriage to Alfonsina Orsini, daughter of Roberto Orsini, Count of Tagliacozzo, and they met in 1488. Their children included Clarice, who married Filippo Strozzi the Younger, and Lorenzo, later Duke of Urbino.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Piero took over as leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1492 on his father's death. The balance among the Italian states that Lorenzo had built collapsed in 1494 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-viii-of-france">Charles VIII of France</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> to assert hereditary claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, lured, according to the account, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a>. Charles asked Piero to support his claim and let his army pass through Tuscany. Piero waited five days and replied that Florence would stay neutral, which was unacceptable to Charles, who sacked and massacred the fortress of Fivizzano.</p><p>Piero received little support from a Florentine elite under the influence of the Dominican Girolamo Savonarola, and even his cousins Lorenzo and Giovanni allied themselves with Charles. By the end of October, without consulting the Signoria, he went to Charles's camp and acceded to all the king's demands, surrendering the fortresses of Sarzana, Pietrasanta, Sarzanello and Librafratta and the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a>. When he reported to the Signoria he met public outrage, and he and his family fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. The family palazzo was looted and the Medici were formally exiled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In exile Piero and his family lived by selling Medici jewels collected by Lorenzo, with help from the French diplomat Philippe de Commines. He tried several times to return, once appearing at the Porta Romana with a band of men who left for Siena when the Florentines did not welcome them. In 1503 he drowned in the Garigliano River while fleeing after the Battle of Garigliano, lost by the French with whom he was allied, and he was buried at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Abbey of Monte Cassino</a> in a tomb designed by Antonio and Battista da Sangallo. No Medici ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> again until 1512, when Giovanni de' Medici forced the city to surrender, and in 1513 he was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piero the Unfortunate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Charles VIII of France invaded Italy in 1494, <b>Piero de’ Medici</b> surrendered fortresses to him and was expelled by the citizens, and the friar Girolamo Savonarola dominated the restored republic until he was burned in 1498.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1492" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Piero di Lorenzo de' Medici"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1492">What was here in 1492? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
